</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Protein-binding domains, including SH2, SH3, and ligand binding domains, contribute to therapeutic intervention by disrupting specific protein-protein interactions involved in excitotoxic pathways. For example, targeting the interaction between NR2B and PSD-95 with peptides like Tat-NR2B9c can mitigate excitotoxicity by uncoupling NMDAR activation from downstream toxic signaling. Additionally, these domains can modulate enzyme active sites and protein dimerization sites, offering multiple avenues for pharmacological intervention in neurological disorders such as ischemic brain injury and neurodegenerative conditions. 

