","The entropy change (ΔS) for a reaction is calculated using the following formula:

ΔS = ΣS_f(products) - ΣS_f(reactants)

where S_f is the standard molar entropy of formation.

Given the reaction and the molar entropies:

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) \]

\[ \text{S(PbS)} = 91.2 \, \text{J/K·mol} \]
\[ \text{S(O}_2\text{)} = 205.1 \, \text{J/K·mol} \]
\[ \text{S(PbO)} = 66.5 \, \text{J/K·mol} \]
\[ \text{S(SO}_2\text{)} = 248.2 \, \text{J/K·mol} \]

Now, we can calculate the entropy change:

ΔS = 2 * S(PbO) + 2 * S(SO2) - (2 * S(PbS) + 3 * S(O2))

ΔS = 2 * (66.5) + 2 * (248.2) - (2 * 91.2 + 3 * 205.1)

ΔS = 133.0 + 496.4 - (182.4 + 615.3)

ΔS = 629.4 - 797.7

