The standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is given as -2220 kJ/mol. This value represents the heat released when 1 mole of propane is completely burned.  

Since we have 2.5 moles of propane, the total heat released is:

\[
\text{Total heat released} = 2.5 \text{ moles} \times 1000 \text{ kJ/mol} = 2500 \text{ kJ}
\]

Thus, the total heat released when 2.5 moles of propane is completely burned is \(\boxed{5550 \text{ kJ}}\). 

**Note**: The correct calculation is \(\boxed{5550 \text{ kJ}}\). \(\boxed{2500 \text{ kJ}}\) is incorrect because it does not account for the given enthalpy of combustion. 

\[
\text{Total heat released} = 2.5 \text{ moles} \times 2220 \text{ kJ/mol} = 5550 \text{ kJ}
\]
