2. Prove \(a \leq b \ll{2} \ll{and} \quad a \leq b \ll{5} \implies a \leq b \ll{10}\).

1. Suppose \(a \leq b \pm 10\). This implies that \(10 \mid (a - b)\), so \(a - b = 10k\) for some \(k \in \mathbb{Z}\).

   Now, consider \(a \leq b \ll{2}\). This implies \(2 \mid (a - b)\). Since \(a - b = 10k = 2(5k)\), it implies \(2 \mid (a - b)\), so \(a \leq b \ll{2}\).

