\[ 2\sin^2 B - \sin^2 C = 2\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) = 1. \]
Since $A + B + C = 180^\circ$, we have $C = 180^\circ - A - B = 180^\circ - 45^\circ - B = 135^\circ - B$. Thus, $\sin C = \sin (135^\circ - B) = \sin 135^\circ \cos B - \cos 135^\circ \sin B = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cos B + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\sin B = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}(\cos B + \sin B)$. Thus, $\sin^2 C = \frac{1}{2}(\cos^2 B + 2\cos B \sin B + \sin^2 B) = \frac{1}{2}(1 + 2\cos B \sin B) = \frac{1}{2} + \sin B \cos B$. Substituting this into the equation, we have
