","(a) The force on the proton due to the electric field is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge and \(E\) is the electric field strength. The force is \(F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}\).

Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), we can find the acceleration:
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]

(b) The formula for velocity as a function of time, assuming constant acceleration, is \(v = v_0 + at\), where \(v_0\) is the initial velocity, \(a\) is the acceleration, and \(t\) is the time. Since the proton starts from rest, \(v_0 = 0\). We want to find the time \(t\) when \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\).

Solving for \(t\):
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s}.
\]

Final Answers:  
(a) The acceleration of the proton is \(4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\).  
