A cylinder contains 2 moles of an ideal gas at an initial pressure of 2 atm and an initial volume of 4L. The gas expands isothermally until its volume becomes 8L. Calculate the work done by the gas during the expansion process.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the work done by the gas during the isothermal expansion, we use the formula for work done in an isothermal process:

\[
W = -nRT \ln\left(\frac{V_2}{V_1}\right)
\]

where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( n = \text{number of moles} \)
- \( R = 8.314 \text{ J/mol·K} = 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K} \) (using L-atm as the pressure and volume units)
- \( T = \text{temperature} \)
- \( V_1 = \text{initial volume} \)
- \( V_2 = \text{final volume} \)

First, we need to find the temperature \( T \). We can use the ideal gas law:

\[
PV = nRT
\]

Substitute the initial conditions:
\[
(2 \text{ atm})(4 \text{ L}) = (2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K}) T
\]
\[
8 = 0.1642T
\]
\[
T = \frac{8}{0.1642} \approx 48.6 \text{ K}
\]

Now, we can calculate the work done:

\[
W = -nRT \ln\left(\frac{V_2}{V_1}\right) = -(2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K})(48.6 \text{ K}) \ln\left(\frac{8 \text{ L}}{4 \text{ L}}\right)
\]
\[
W = -(2 \times 0.0821 \times 48.6) \ln(2) \text{ L·atm}
\]
\[
W = -(1.642 \times 48.6) \times 0.693 \text{ L·atm}
\]
\[
W = -79.87 \times 0.693 \text{ L·atm}
\]
\[
W \approx -55.3 \text{ L·atm}
\]

To convert the work to joules, we can use the conversion factor \( 1 \text{ L·atm} = 101.325 \text{ J} \):
\[
W = -55.3 \text{ L·atm} \times 101.325 \text{ J/L·atm} \approx -5610 \text{ J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is approximately -55.3 L·atm or -5610 J. The negative sign indicates that the work is done by the system (expansion).

**Final Answer:**
The work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{-55.3 \text{ L·atm}}\) or \(\boxed{-5610 \text{ J}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
