To find the curl of the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \mathbf{i} P(x, y) + \mathbf{j} Q(x, y)\), we use the following form of the curl:
\[
\mathbf{C} = \nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial R}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial R}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Here, \(\mathbf{V} = P \mathbf{i} + Q \mathbf{j} + R \mathbf{k}\), so in this case, \(R = 0\). Substituting \(R = 0\) into the curl formula, we get:
\[
\mathbf{C} = \left( \frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial 0}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Simplifying, we get:
\[
\mathbf{C} = \left( 0 - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial z} - 0 \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Since \(\mathbf{V}\) only depends on \(x\) and \(y\), the partial derivatives with respect to \(z\) are zero, so \(\frac{\partial P}{\partial z} = 0\) and \(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} = 0\). Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\mathbf{C} = 0 \cdot \mathbf{i} + 0 \cdot \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl is:
\[
\mathbf{C} = \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]

Final Answer: \(\boxed{\left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}\).
