- \( c \) is the speed of light in vacuum (\( \boxed{3 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}} \))
- \( h \) is the Planck constant (\( \boxed{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J·s}} \))
- \( k \) is the Boltzmann constant (\( \boxed{1.381 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K}} \))
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation.
- \( T \) is the temperature of the body (in Kelvin).

### Explanation of the terms:

- The term \( \frac{1}{e^{hc/kT} - 1} \) accounts for the quantum nature of radiation.
- The term \( \frac{1}{\lambda^5} \) is a power function of the wavelength, which is a logarithmic curve.
- The exponential term \( e^{-hc/\lambda kT} \) describes the decrease in intensity as wavelength increases.

