","To compute the natural frequencies and mode levels of the three-blade disc system, we can use the mass and stiffness matrices. The system is coupled, so we need to solve an eigenvalues and eigenvectors problem.  

1. **Mass Matrix**:  
   Since all masses are unchanged, the mass matrix \( M \) is diagonal:  
   \[
   M = \begin{bmatrix} 0.0114 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0.0114 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0.0114 \end{bmatrix} \, \text{kg}
   \]

2. **Stibility Stiffness Matrix**:  
   The stiffness matrix \( K \) is composed of the individual shaft stiffness \( k_t \) and the coupling stiffness \( K_c \):  
   \[
   K = \begin{pmatrix}
   k_t + 2K_c & -K_c & -K_c \\
   -K_c & k_t + 2K_c & -K_c \\
   -K_c & -K_c & k_t + 2K_c
   \end{bmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
   430,000 + 2 \times 45,430 & -45,430 & -45,430 \\
   -45,430 & 430,000 + 2 \times 45,430 & -44,430 \\
   -45,430 & -45,430 & 430,000 + 2 \times 45,430
   \end{bmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
   520,860 & -45,430 & -45,430 \\
   -45,430 & 520,860 | -45,430 \\
   -45,430 & -45,430 & 520,860
   \end{bmatrix} \, \text{N/m}
   \]

3. **Eigenvalue Analysis**:  
   The eigenvalues \( \lambda_i \) and eigenvectors \( v_i \) are found by solving the equation:  
   \[
   \text{det}(K - \lambda M) = 0
   \]  
